{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792335144,"nonce":"sLzD4WJLaSuYk9cLT4R7uITot3w=","title":"Opening the command center again while it is closing aborts the program","message":"Close the center (Esc, or choosing a command), then open it again a few frames later, before its closing animation has played, and close it once more: the native layout engine panics with \"invalid SlotMap key used\" (taffy, tree/taffy_tree.rs:621) and the process aborts. A person can do this by pressing Esc and then Cmd+P quickly.\n\nReproduced offscreen in the snapshots' basic scenario: close, settle 3 frames, open, settle 30, close, settle 40. The abort comes in Offscreen.advance, from Element.remove in an animation callback.\n\nWhat the code does: ashui's Floating calls TopEntry.close, which hands onClose back at once (so Floating's entry is null) and removes the layer when the closing animation has played. Opening again meanwhile makes a second entry and moves the panel into a new layer; the first entry's finish then removes its layer. Something in that handoff removes a node twice. Opening in the same frame as the close, which choosing a command that opens a picker does, is fine; so is opening after the animation.\n\nWorked around in RayDE: Workbench.showCenter holds a request made during the closing back until the animation has played (the animation clock, the theme's durationFaster plus a margin), and a later request replaces one that waits. The snapshots drive the quick close and open and check the center ends up open.\n\nOpen: the cause is in ashui's top layer, which should survive this on its own; reported to ashui. When it does, showCenter can go.","files":null}]}